## Identified Problems & Solutions

### 1. `server.listen` Missing Explicit `'0.0.0.0'` Binding (BLOCKER)

#### Problem
In [`server/src/server.js:218`](file:///c:/Users/Ashwin/Desktop/LMS/server/src/server.js#L218), the server starts with:
```javascript
server.listen(PORT, () => { ... });
```
Render web services run inside isolated Docker containers behind an internal NGINX reverse proxy. If Node's HTTP server is not explicitly bound to host `'0.0.0.0'`, the operating system defaults to binding to `127.0.0.1` (localhost). Render's port detection mechanism will fail to receive traffic from the public proxy, causing the deployment to time out with:
`==> Port 10000 not responding, deployment failed`.

#### Solution
Update line 218 in [`server/src/server.js`](file:///c:/Users/Ashwin/Desktop/LMS/server/src/server.js) to explicitly bind to `'0.0.0.0'`:
```javascript
server.listen(PORT, '0.0.0.0', () => { ... });
```

---

### 2. Hardcoded Database Credentials in Fallbacks (BLOCKER / SECURITY)

#### Problem
In [`server/src/utils/prisma.js:14,46`](file:///c:/Users/Ashwin/Desktop/LMS/server/src/utils/prisma.js#L14-L46), fallback connection strings containing real database credentials are hardcoded in the source file:
```javascript
const pgUrl = process.env.POSTGRES_URL || ... || 'postgresql://neondb_owner:...';
const mongoUrl = process.env.MONGODB_URL || ... || 'mongodb+srv://...';
```
If the repository is pushed to a remote git provider (GitHub, GitLab), these production connection credentials are exposed. If an environment variable is mistyped on Render, the server might silently connect to a development database instead of failing visibly.

#### Solution
Remove hardcoded credentials from source code. Require connection URLs to be provided via environment variables (`DATABASE_URL`, `POSTGRES_URL`, `MONGODB_URL`), logging a clean diagnostic error if missing in production.

